    body: "# What I got wrong\n\nOne running file. One line per surprise. All ninety days.\n\n**Nobody reads this but you.** It isn't graded, it doesn't go into any review, and the\nblunter it is the more it's worth.\n\n---\n\n## Why bother\n\nTwo reasons, and the second is the real one.\n\n**1. Patterns show up that no single project reveals.** Any one wrong prediction is just a\nthing you didn't know yet. Four of them together tend to show a *habit* — you keep trusting\nthe platform number, or you keep assuming two accounts behave alike because they sell similar\nthings. A habit is fixable. But you can't fix one you can't see, and you won't see it from\nmemory.\n\n**2. It's the evidence at Day 90.** When you re-score\n[`baseline.md`](../../05-self-assessment/baseline.md), this file is the difference between\n\"I think I improved\" and knowing exactly where and why. It's also the answer to \"what turned\nout to be harder than you expected,\" which is one of the two questions waiting for you there.\n\n---\n\n## How to keep it\n\nA minute per project, right after the write-up. Suggested shape — but any shape you'll\nactually maintain beats the one you won't:\n\n```markdown\n## T1-1 · Account tour — 14 Mar\n\n**I predicted:** mostly search, one PMax, brand a small share of spend.\n**Actually:** three PMax campaigns, and brand was a much bigger share than I guessed.\n**Why I was off:** I assumed PMax was a small experiment. It's most of how this\naccount runs, and I'd have known that from the primer if I'd connected it.\n**The habit to watch:** I default to search being the main thing because it's what\nI learned first.\n```\n\nThe last line is the one that earns its place. **The gap is data; the habit behind it is the\nlesson.**\n\n---\n\n## Three things worth writing down even though they'll feel small\n\n- **When you were right for the wrong reason.** Easy to miss and just as useful — a correct\n  answer from a broken model will fail you later, on something bigger.\n- **When you didn't have a prediction at all.** \"No idea\" is a legitimate starting point, and\n  the day you *do* have an instinct about that same thing is a real, datable milestone.\n- **When someone corrected you and you didn't follow why.** That one's a question, not a\n  gap — put it in `../questions/` too, and come back to it.\n\n---\n\n## One thing to be clear about\n\nBeing wrong a lot in your first months is the plan working, not the plan failing. Every\nproject built on predict-then-compare **expects** the gap — if there's no gap, the project\nwas too easy and we scoped it badly.\n\nA long file here at Day 90 is a good outcome. A short one usually means the predictions were\nbeing written after the fact."
